<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page3-elimination-and-threats" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Elimination Revisited, and Threats and Promises Again</title>

  <p>
    Earlier we saw that in the Prisoner's Dilemma, <m>\text{talk}</m> strictly
    dominates <m>\text{refuse}</m> for both executives, so eliminating dominated
    strategies would have landed on <m>(\text{talk}, \text{talk})</m>, which is
    exactly the Nash equilibrium we found by inspection. In Section 3.6, you
    will read some theorems showing this is not a coincidence!! However, the
    connection between Nash equilibria and iterated elimination also has some
    nuances to pay attention to.
  </p>



  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-6">
    <title>Section 3.6, Finding Nash Equilibria by Iterated Elimination of
    Dominated Strategies</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read everything <em>before</em> the proof in Section 3.6. Reading
      the proof is optional. If you are a math major or particularly interested
      in game theory, you should read the proof.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      You'll be reading about connections between iterated elimination and
      Nash equilibria in Theorem 3.1 (for iterated elimination of weakly
      dominated strategies) and then in Theorem 3.2 (for iterated elimination
      of strictly dominated strategies). Pay attention to the similarities and
      differences between the statements.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    In both theorems, we take a game <m>G</m>, run iterated elimination on it,
    and call the reduced game we end up with <m>H</m>. The main question is
    whether Nash equilibria are preserved along the way, which really splits
    into two questions:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        Is every Nash equilibrium of <m>H</m> a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>?
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        Is every Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m> a Nash equilibrium of <m>H</m>?
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The answers depend on whether you eliminate weakly or strictly dominated
    strategies! After reading through the statements of Theorems 3.1 and 3.2,
    answer the following:
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-strict-vs-weak-loss">
    <statement>
      <p>
        You run iterated elimination on a game <m>G</m> and get a reduced game
        <m>H</m>. What is the difference between having used strict dominance
        and having used weak dominance?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            With weak dominance, <m>H</m> may contain a Nash equilibrium that
            was not a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite, and this is the direction that is safe in both cases.
            Theorem 3.1 says explicitly that every Nash equilibrium of <m>H</m>
            is also one of <m>G</m>, weak dominance included. The danger runs
            the other way: an equilibrium of <m>G</m> can go missing.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Either way, every Nash equilibrium of the reduced game <m>H</m> is
            also a Nash equilibrium of the original game <m>G</m>. But with
            weak dominance, <m>G</m> may have equilibria that no longer appear
            in <m>H</m>, while with strict dominance it cannot.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. Both theorems give you the <q>nothing false is
            introduced</q> direction. Only strict dominance gives you the
            <q>nothing is lost</q> direction: by statement (2) of Theorem 3.2,
            an equilibrium of <m>G</m> never uses an eliminated strategy, so it
            is still in <m>H</m>, and it is still an equilibrium there because
            players have fewer strategies to deviate to.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            There is no real difference: either way, <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> have
            exactly the same Nash equilibria.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That is true for strict dominance, but not for weak dominance.
            Weak elimination can delete an equilibrium of <m>G</m> along the
            way, and you will see exactly this happen with Big Monkey and
            Little Monkey in Section 3.7.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-strategy-vs-profile">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Statement (2) of Theorem 3.2 says that each strategy eliminated in the
        course of iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies is not
        part of any Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>. What does that let you
        conclude about a strategy profile <m>s = (s_1, \ldots, s_n)</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            If even one player's strategy <m>s_i</m> was eliminated, then the
            whole profile <m>s</m> fails to be a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>,
            no matter what the other players are doing.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct. A single eliminated component is enough to disqualify the
            entire profile, which is why statement (2) is such a strong claim.
            It is also what lets us conclude that every Nash equilibrium of
            <m>G</m> is built entirely out of surviving strategies, and so is
            still sitting there in <m>H</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            If <m>s</m> survives to the reduced game <m>H</m>, then <m>s</m> is
            a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            That is not statement (2), and it is not true either. Surviving
            elimination does not make a profile an equilibrium; plenty of
            profiles in <m>H</m> are not equilibria of anything. Statement (3)
            is the one about profiles, and it applies to Nash equilibria of
            <m>H</m>, not to every profile of <m>H</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="check-single-profile-conclusion">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose iterated elimination of <em>strictly</em> dominated strategies
        reduces a game <m>G</m> all the way down to a single strategy profile
        <m>(s_1^*, \ldots, s_n^*)</m>. What can you conclude?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, but <m>G</m> may well have
            others that the elimination missed.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            This is the right conclusion for <em>weak</em> elimination, not
            strict. Under strict elimination nothing gets missed, since
            statement (2) rules out the eliminated strategies appearing in any
            equilibrium.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is the unique Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, though it need not
            be a strict one.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Uniqueness is right, but you get strictness for free here as well.
            Every alternative strategy was eliminated, and the comparisons
            behind a strict elimination are all strict inequalities.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            It is a strict Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>, and <m>G</m> has no
            other Nash equilibria.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the best possible outcome of a reduction. The
            <q>no others</q> half comes from statement (2): any other
            equilibrium would have to use a strategy that got eliminated, and
            those cannot appear in equilibria at all.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    And here is a quick summary of some of the conclusions!
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        With either kind of elimination, every Nash equilibrium of <m>H</m> is
        a Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m>. Elimination never introduces a false
        equilibrium.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        With strict elimination, every Nash equilibrium of <m>G</m> also
        survives to <m>H</m>, so <m>G</m> and <m>H</m> have exactly the same
        Nash equilibria. With weak elimination, equilibria of <m>G</m> can be
        lost along the way.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        If strict elimination leaves a single profile, it is the unique Nash
        equilibrium of <m>G</m>, and a strict one.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    <alert>Weak elimination can lose you equilibria; strict elimination
    cannot.</alert>
  </p>

  <p>
    Now we apply these theorems to one of our favorite games :)
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-3-7">
    <title>Section 3.7, Big Monkey and Little Monkey 4: Threats, Promises,
    and Commitments Revisited</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Section 3.7.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      This is a short section that connects the elimination of weakly
      dominated strategies in the normal form of Big Monkey and Little Monkey
      to its Nash equilibria.
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <p>
    Here is the matrix again for reference, with Big Monkey moving first, as it
    appeared in Section 2.10 and in the two elimination interactives from
    Chapter 2.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-bmlm-normal-form-3-7">
    <title>Big Monkey and Little Monkey in normal form, Big Monkey first</title>
    <tabular halign="center">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell></cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: ww</cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: wc</cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: cw</cell>
        <cell>Little Monkey: cc</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Big Monkey: w</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(0, 0)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
        <cell>(9, 1)</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell>Big Monkey: c</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
        <cell>(4, 4)</cell>
        <cell>(5, 3)</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <p>
    Some of the Nash equilibria here can be deduced from the reduced games
    found in Section 2.11 of the textbook. That is, <m>(w, cw)</m> and
    <m>(w, cc)</m> are Nash equilibria of the reduced games from Section 2.11,
    so by Theorem 3.1 they are guaranteed to be Nash equilibria of the original
    game. However, <m>(c, ww)</m> is another Nash equilibrium!! It did not
    appear in either reduced game, so this is exactly the kind of equilibrium
    weak elimination can lose.
  </p>

  <p>
    The book verifies <m>(c, ww)</m> for us in words, and its argument needs
    reasoning for both players:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>
        If Little Monkey commits in advance to waiting no matter what Big
        Monkey does, then Big Monkey can do no better than to climb.
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        If Big Monkey climbs, then Little Monkey can do no better than to wait,
        which is what <m>ww</m> has her do.
      </p>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Those two sentences together are the definition of a Nash equilibrium
    applied to this profile. To practice this reasoning, try it out for the
    other two equilibria in the following example!
  </p>

  <example xml:id="example-verify-w-cc-w-cw">
    <title>Verifying the other two equilibria</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        For each profile, give the argument that it is a Nash equilibrium: one
        statement about Big Monkey's incentive and one about Little Monkey's,
        in the same style as the book's argument for <m>(c, ww)</m>. Cite the
        payoffs from the matrix above.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="verify-w-cc">
      <title>The promise, <m>(w, cc)</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>(w, cc)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If Little Monkey commits in advance to climbing no matter what
              Big Monkey does, then Big Monkey can do no better than to wait:
              waiting pays him <m>9</m> and climbing pays him <m>5</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If Big Monkey waits, then Little Monkey can do no better than to
              climb, which is what <m>cc</m> has her do: across the <m>w</m>
              row her payoffs are <m>0, 0, 1, 1</m>, and <m>cc</m> gives her
              one of the two best.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="verify-w-cw">
      <title>Backward induction's answer, <m>(w, cw)</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Show that <m>(w, cw)</m> is a Nash equilibrium. Then compare your
          argument to the one for <m>(w, cc)</m>: what changed?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              If Little Monkey commits in advance to <m>cw</m>, then Big Monkey
              can do no better than to wait: waiting pays him <m>9</m> and
              climbing pays him <m>4</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              If Big Monkey waits, then Little Monkey can do no better than
              <m>cw</m>: across the <m>w</m> row her payoffs are
              <m>0, 0, 1, 1</m>, and <m>cw</m> gives her one of the two best.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The only thing that changed is Big Monkey's alternative payoff,
          <m>4</m> instead of <m>5</m>, and either way it loses to <m>9</m>.
          Little Monkey's half is identical: the same row, the same payoffs,
          and the same tie between <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </example>

  <p>
    Notice <m>(w, cw)</m> and
    <m>(w, cc)</m> are two different strategy profiles, but they
    pay both players identically and their verifications are nearly the same
    argument. What distinguishes them?
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="check-off-path">
    <statement>
      <p>
        What is the actual difference between Little Monkey's strategies
        <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>, given that Big Monkey is playing <m>w</m>?
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="no">
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            They differ in what Little Monkey does when Big Monkey waits, which
            is the branch that actually gets played.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Not quite; look at the labels again. Both <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>
            begin with <m>c</m>, which is Little Monkey's move in response to
            Big Monkey waiting. It is the second letter, her response to Big
            Monkey climbing, where they part ways.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement>
          <p>
            They differ only in what Little Monkey would do if Big Monkey
            climbed. Big Monkey does not climb, so that part of her strategy is
            never carried out and the two strategies produce identical play.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct, and this is the heart of Section 3.7. A strategy has to
            specify a move at <em>every</em> node, including nodes the game
            never actually reaches. Little Monkey's promise costs her nothing
            precisely because it is never tested. This is also the honest
            answer to the book's question of why the normal form has equilibria
            that backward induction does not find, and it is an idea we will
            return to in a later chapter.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement>
          <p>
            Little Monkey prefers one of them, so she would choose that one if
            given the chance.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            She is indifferent. Both give her <m>1</m> when Big Monkey waits.
            If she strictly preferred one of them, the other could not have
            been a Nash equilibrium in the first place.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <p>
    Note that none of these three equilibria is strict, and Little Monkey's
    payoffs are the reason. In the <m>w</m> row she is indifferent between
    <m>cw</m> and <m>cc</m>; in the <m>c</m> row she is indifferent between
    <m>ww</m> and <m>cw</m>. She is never made strictly worse off by deviating.
  </p>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-3-3" iframe="question-form.html?section=3.3" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>